# SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000194  -  CAT II
# The web server must interpret and normalize ambiguous HTTP requests or
# terminate the TCP connection.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000194
group:           V-264364
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-001310
control_rev5:    SI-10

contribution:    Splunk runs its own HTTP listeners, so the product can withhold on each
                 one the HTTP/1.1 features the Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding desync
                 depends on (forceHttp10) and reject a request whose content length exceeds
                 a bound (max_content_length on the splunkd REST listener, max_upload_size
                 on Splunk Web); it exposes no setting that normalizes an ambiguous
                 request, none that drops an unnormalizable request and closes the
                 connection, and no HTTP/2 listener option.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           server.conf [httpServer] forceHttp10 not_equals never
check:           server.conf [httpServer] max_content_length max 2147483648
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] forceHttp10 https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          server.conf [httpServer] max_content_length https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] forceHttp10 https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] max_upload_size https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] forceHttp10 not_equals never
                 reason: "forceHttp10 = auto|never|always. How the HTTP server deals with
                         HTTP/1.0 support for incoming clients. When set to 'always', the
                         REST HTTP server does not use some HTTP 1.1 features such as
                         persistent connections or chunked transfer encoding. When set to
                         'auto', it limits HTTP 1.1 features only if the client sent no
                         User-Agent header, or if the user agent is known to have bugs in
                         its HTTP/1.1 support. When set to 'never', it always allows HTTP
                         1.1, even to clients it suspects might be buggy. Default: auto"
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] max_upload_size max 500
                 reason: "The hard maximum limit, in megabytes, of uploaded files. Default:
                         500"
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Normalization of an ambiguous request, and the drop-and-terminate behavior
                 the fix text directs for requests that cannot be normalized: no Splunk
                 setting exists for either, so this half of the objective is carried by the
                 reverse proxy or load balancer fronting Splunk and is assessed against
                 that component's own baseline.
external:        The HTTP/2-only front-end to back-end path that the requirement's
                 discussion names as the complete remedy for all smuggling variants: Splunk
                 exposes no HTTP/2 listener setting, so it is carried by the front-end web
                 tier.
external:        Whether a front end is deployed at all cannot be read from Splunk
                 configuration. Where one is, web.conf [settings] tools.proxy.on with a
                 non-empty tools.proxy.base makes Splunk treat the front end's
                 X-Forwarded-* headers as authoritative (web.conf.spec:1016-1032); because
                 its correct value depends on the deployment topology rather than on the
                 product, it is recorded here instead of being made a condition that would
                 fail a directly exposed instance.

citation:        SRG-APP-000251-WSR-000194 check, fix and discussion text (CCI-001310,
                 SI-10); no product STIG rule shares CCI-001310. Splunk 10.x specification
                 set (Version 10.0.0): server.conf.spec:1416 forceHttp10 within stanza
                 [httpServer] at server.conf.spec:1291, default auto; server.conf.spec:1369
                 max_content_length, default 2147483648 bytes, documented as rejecting HTTP
                 requests over the configured size; web.conf.spec:1129 forceHttp10, default
                 auto, documented as withholding HTTP/1.1 features including chunked
                 transfer encoding when set to always; web.conf.spec:867 max_upload_size,
                 default 500 MB; web.conf.spec:1016 tools.proxy.on and web.conf.spec:1031
                 tools.proxy.base.
finding:         Splunk Enterprise is not configured to the fullest extent the product
                 allows against ambiguous HTTP requests — forceHttp10 is set to never in
                 server.conf [httpServer] or web.conf [settings], or max_content_length or
                 max_upload_size is raised above the vendor default bound — so a listener
                 retains the HTTP/1.1 features and the unbounded message body on which
                 request smuggling depends; this is assessed at the application layer
                 alone, and ambiguous-request normalization, the drop-and-close behavior
                 and the HTTP/2 front-end path are recorded as external dependencies
                 carried by the reverse proxy or load balancer, not as part of this
                 finding.
